body:
> ... "Does it make sense to map devices and players ahead of time in some settings, when devices can connect amd disconnect at any time?.

I completely agree that these settings need to be changeable at runtime. We will want to support swapping player IDs (e.g. second player wants to become player 1), as well as players joining/leaving, like you said.

> Another issue, I guess, is that now through InputMap you can have for the same action multiple inputs from different devices. But when you scale it to multiple players, it's not like the other players will have their own keyboards. So there's this default situation where player 0 has its InputMap with both keyboard and controller, then the other players only have the controller inputs. Should we clarify (and how) that if you assign to an action in InputMap both keyboard and controller inputs, the keyboard will only work for the first player? And what if I wanted to give the keyboard to player 2 , and controller to player 1?

First, just to clarify, the keyboard doesn't always have to be player 1 - it can be reassigned.

Now, if one action in the Input Map has both keyboard and joypad events, those events should be the default binding _for that device type_, regardless of which player is inputting the action. This means that joypad events in the Input Map should _only match against `All Devices`, not specific device IDs_. We could consider showing a warning if a joypad event has a specific device ID, but I don't think we can do anything else - the Input Map has to continue to work as it does now for backwards compatibility.

> ... Also, keyboard, mouse and the first connected controller are always (?) defined by a device with index 0". We probably need to clarify better how this should work, and also have some new API for the developers to control this mapping stuff at runtime...
> ... Can we do something about subdividing the kb, mouse, controller 1 having all index 0?

At a high level, we need a way for the developer to specify that certain device_type+device ID pairs belong to a certain player. For backwards compatibility, all device type+device ID pairs should default to player 1. Therefore, we need a data structure that, for each device type, maps from device ID -> player ID. That way engine code can look up "type=joypad + ID=3 -> Player 3" or "type=touch + ID=0 -> Player 1".

I suggest we start simple for now and group non-joypad device types together, meaning touch, mouse, keyboard, and emulated mouse will all be assigned to the same player. This creates two device categories: joypads and "everything else". Something like the following should work:

```cpp
// godot/core/input/input_map.h

// ...

private:
	// ...

	// NOTE: This only stores device ID -> PlayerId mappings for non-
	// `PlayerId::P1` players; if device is missing then assume player 1.
	HashMap<int, PlayerId> device_id_to_player_override_joy;

	PlayerId kbm_device_player_id_override = PlayerID::P1;
```

```cpp
// godot/core/input/input_map.cpp

// ...

void InputMap::set_player_for_joy_device(int p_device, PlayerId p_player) {
	// TODO: Assert valid player ID.

	if (p_player == PlayerId::P1 && device_id_to_player_joy.has(p_device)) {
		// NOTE: If no mapping set
		device_id_to_player_joy.erase(p_device);
	} else if (p_player != PlayerId::P1) {
		device_id_to_player_joy.set(p_device, p_player);
	}
}

PlayerId InputMap::get_player_for_joy_device(int p_device) {
	if (!device_id_to_player_joy.has(p_device)) {
		return PlayerId::P1;
	}

	return device_id_to_player_joy.get(p_device);
}

void InputMap::set_player_for_kbm_devices(PlayerId p_player) {
	// TODO: Assert valid player ID.
	kbm_device_player_id_override = p_player;
}

PlayerId InputMap::get_player_for_kbm_devices() {
	return kbm_device_player_id_override;
}
```

A few things to note:
 - We can optimize the mapping data structures, but starting with `HashMap` is simplest.
 - In the future, we can split up mouse, keyboard, touch, and emulated mouse input devices to be separately assignable to players.
 - We'll probably want to store `device_id_to_player_override_joy` and `kbm_device_player_id_override` within `ProjectSettings` so developers can specify overrides via the settings UI (just a simple setting, not a custom UI), but let's ignore that for now.

