I probably found a better way to map joypads to players.
I implemented an override for the keyboard and mouse player in `ProjectSetting`, but then I tried to map the player for the controller directly in the `Joypad` struct. It seems to work well and doesn't introduces complexities like having to synchronize a `HashMap` element when a joypad disconnects, connects and all that kind of stuff.
```cpp
// input.h

struct Joypad {
    StringName name;
    StringName uid;
    PlayerId player_id = PlayerId::P1;
    // ...
};
```
Mapping a joypad to a player in GDScript is very straightforward:
```gdscript
Input.set_joy_player_id(0, PLAYER_ID_P2)
```
I thought a little bit about mapping devices and players in the UI, but I feel like it is almost useless, since the behaviour of joypad connecting/disconnecting is completely dinamic and realtime. Having it hardcoded at the start doesn't feel very useful: for now each connected device just maps its devide id to the corresponding player id, since they also start from 0 it works well.
E.g. device_id = 0 -> player = 0 (P1)
Then I can connect a function to the joypad connected signal and bind it like I want, eventually.

