Thank you, now I see how it works!
Given what you said, I decided to just encapsulate the `action_states` in another `HashMap`, and with little code it seems to work.
```cpp
// Key -> player_id.
// Value -> All available actions to that player.
HashMap<int, HashMap<StringName, ActionState>> action_states;
```
I added a new scene to the test project that tests if it's possible to trigger the same action but with different players, and it works.
Now if player 1 presses "ui_select", and keep it pressed, player 2 can press its "ui_select" and the event will still be correctly triggered: they are now completely separated.
The previous behaviour is preserved, so, if you trigger "ui_select" with both the joypad 0 (assigned to player 1) and the keyboard (assigned to player 1) only the first press counts.
